hadiths[80]{hadithnumber,text}:
"5929","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنه) narrated while circumambulating the Ka'bah, through Abu Abdillah his freedman. Abadah, one of the narrators, explained that the days intended are the first ten of Dhul-Hijjah. > **""There are no days in which deeds are more beloved to Allah than in these days. It was said: Not even jihad in the path of Allah? He said: Not even jihad in the path of Allah, except a man who goes out with his life and his wealth, then does not return until his lifeblood is shed.""**"
"5930","The narrator was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when deeds were mentioned, and he spoke concerning the ten days, with the remainder of the narration like the previous one. > **""There are no days in which deeds are more beloved to Allah than in these ten.""**"
"5931","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning the ten days. > **""There are no days in which deeds are superior to the days of the ten. It was said: Not even jihad in the path of Allah? He said: Not even jihad in the path of Allah.""**"
"5932","Abdullah ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning the ten days of Dhul-Hijjah. > **""There are no days greater before Allah, nor is deeds therein more beloved to Allah, than the days of the ten, so increase therein in tasbih, tahlil, tahmid, and takbir.""**"
"5933","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the ten intended are the ten of Dhul-Hijjah. When asked whether even the like of them spent in the path of Allah does not equal them, he excepted the one who blackens his face in dust, meaning the martyr. He then mentioned the Day of Arafah as a day of boasting, with the full lengthy narration as previously recorded. > **""The best days of the world are the days of the ten. It was said: Not even the like of them in the path of Allah? He said: Not even the like of them in the path of Allah, except a man who blackens his face in the dust. The Day of Arafah is a day of boasting.""**"
"5934","Abu Sa'id (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed Fatimah (رضي الله عنها). When she asked whether this was exclusive to Ahl al-Bayt or for them and the Muslims, he clarified its generality. > **""O Fatimah, stand by your sacrifice and witness it, for with every drop that falls from its blood, your past sins will be forgiven for you. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, is this for us of Ahl al-Bayt exclusively, or for us and for the Muslims? He said: Rather, for us and for the Muslims.""**"
"5935","Imran ibn Husayn (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded Fatimah (رضي الله عنها) to stand and witness her sacrifice, informing her that every sin she had committed would be forgiven with its first drop of blood, and instructing her to recite the verse of devotion. When Imran asked whether this was exclusive to him ﷺ and his household, who are worthy of it, or general for the Muslims, he declared it general. > **""O Fatimah, stand and witness your sacrifice, for with the first drop of its blood every sin you committed will be forgiven for you. And say: Indeed my prayer and my sacrifice and my living and my dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds, He has no partner, and with that I have been commanded, and I am of the Muslims. This is not for me and my household exclusively, but rather for the Muslims generally.""**"
"5936","Ali (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ addressing the people concerning sacrifice and hoping for its reward, for though the blood falls to the earth, it falls into the protection of Allah Almighty, meaning its reward is recorded. > **""O people, offer sacrifice and hope for reward through its blood, for though the blood falls to the earth, it falls into the protection of Allah Almighty.""**"
"5937","Hasan ibn Ali (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning one who sacrifices with a willing soul while hoping for the reward of his sacrifice. > **""Whoever sacrifices with a willing soul, hoping for the reward of his sacrifice, it will be a veil for him from the Fire.""**"
"5938","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning spending wealth and what is most beloved to Allah among expenditures. > **""No silver you spend is more beloved to Allah than blood shed as sacrifice on the day of Eid.""**"
"5939","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke on the day of sacrifice concerning the best deed of the son of Adam on that day. > **""No son of Adam does on this day any deed superior to blood that is shed, except joining kinship.""**"
"5940","Habib ibn Mikhnaf (رضي الله عنه) reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ on the Day of Arafah while he ﷺ was saying: Do you recognize it? The narrator did not know what the people answered him. > **""Upon the people of every house is to slaughter a sheep in every Rajab, and in every Adha a sheep.""**"
"5941","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade atirah, which was a slaughter they used to slaughter in Rajab, so he forbade them from it and commanded them with the sacrifice of Adha. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade atirah, and commanded the udhiyah.""**"
"5942","Hudhayfah ibn Usayd (رضي الله عنه) reported that he saw Abu Bakr and Umar (رضي الله عنهما) not sacrificing, out of fear that they would be imitated in it as an established obligation. His family then pressed him harshly, though he knew the Sunnah, until he sacrificed on behalf of each person."
"5943","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning the light-coloured, afra animal compared to two black ones. > **""The blood of the afra is more beloved to Allah than the blood of two black ones.""**"
"5944","Kabirah bint Sufyan (رضي الله عنها), who lived in Jahiliyyah and was among those who gave allegiance, reported that she told the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that she had buried four sons of hers alive in Jahiliyyah, so he commanded her to free four slaves. She freed Abu Sa'id and his two sons Maysarah and Jubayr, and Umm Muyassar. She further reported his statement on the sacrifice. > **""Free four slaves. The blood of the afra is purer before Allah than the blood of two black ones.""**"
"5945","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the jadh' of sheep compared to the sayyid of goats. Dawud, a narrator, explained that sayyid means the great and sturdy. > **""The jadh' of sheep is better than the sayyid of goats.""**"
"5946","Abu Hurayrah (رضi الله عنه) reported that Jibril (عليه السلام) came to the Prophet ﷺ on the Day of Sacrifice, so the Prophet ﷺ asked him how he viewed their rite. He replied that the people of heaven boast through it, and informed him that the jadh' of sheep is better than the sayyid of goats, and better than the sayyid of cattle and camels, and that had Allah known anything superior, He would have ransomed Ibrahim (عليه السلام) with it. > **""How do you view our rite? The people of heaven boast through it. And know, O Muhammad, that the jadh' of sheep is better than the sayyid of goats. And know, O Muhammad, that the jadh' of sheep is better than the sayyid of cattle and camels. Had Allah the Blessed and Exalted known anything superior to it, He would have ransomed Ibrahim with it.""**"
"5947","Hudhayfah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded careful examination of the eye and the ear of the sacrificial animal. > **""Examine closely the eye and the ear.""**"
"5948","Ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbidding sacrifice with the muqabalah, the mudabarah, the sharqa, and the kharqa in the eye and ear, meaning animals with portions cut or pierced in the ear or eye. > **""We do not sacrifice the muqabalah, nor the mudabarah, nor the sharqa, nor the kharqa of the eye and ear.""**"
"5949","Abu Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the blind, the emaciated, the mangy, and the one whose udders are wholly cut off are not permissible among sacrificial camels and cattle. Atba, with unpointed letters, means udders, that is, cut off. > **""Not permissible among sacrificial animals are the blind, nor the emaciated, nor the mangy, nor the one whose udders are cut off.""**"
"5950","Abdullah ibn Zayd (رضي الله عنه) reported that he and a man of the Ansar were present with the Prophet ﷺ at the place of slaughter. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed sacrificial animals, but neither he nor his companion received anything. He then shaved his head into his garment and gave it, and it was distributed among men. He trimmed his nails and gave his companion some of his hair. The narrator stated that hair remains with them, dyed with henna and katam."
"5951","Abdullah ibn Zayd (رضي الله عنه) reported in another narration that he and a man of Quraysh were present with the Prophet ﷺ at the place of slaughter while he was distributing sacrifices, but neither he nor his companion received anything. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then shaved his head into a garment and gave it, and it was distributed among men, like the previous narration."
"5952","Umm Bilal (رضي الله عنها) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning sacrificing the jadh' of sheep. > **""Sacrifice the jadh' of sheep, for it is permissible.""**"
"5953","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ sent sheep to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (رضي الله عنه) to distribute among his Companions while they were performing tamattu'. A he-goat remained from them, so Sa'd sacrificed it in his tamattu'."
"5954","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (رضي الله عنه) a jadh' of goats and commanded him to sacrifice it."
"5955","Muhammad ibn Sirin reported that Imran ibn Husayn (رضي الله عنه) said that sacrificing a young animal is more beloved to him than sacrificing an aged one, for the young is more entitled to youthfulness and generosity."
"5956","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that they were sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a man came with a fat, sturdy jadh' of goats and a lean, inferior jadh' of sheep. He asked which of them was better and whether he should sacrifice the goat, so the Prophet ﷺ permitted him, for to Allah belongs what is best. > **""Sacrifice it, for to Allah belongs what is best.""**"
"5957","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning partnership in sacrifice. > **""The cow is for seven, and the camel is for seven, in sacrifices.""**"
"5958","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ joined his Companions on the Day of Hudaybiyah, seven to a cow."
"5959","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ joined between his wives in one cow at the sacrifice."
"5960","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning the camel in sacrifice. > **""The camel in sacrifice is for ten.""**"
"5961","Hasan ibn Ali (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded them to wear the best they could find, to perfume themselves with the best they could find, to sacrifice the fattest they could find, with the cow for seven and the camel for ten, and to come forth with tranquillity and dignity. > **""Wear the best you can find, perfume yourselves with the best you can find, and sacrifice the fattest you can find. The cow is for seven, and the camel is for ten. Come forth with tranquillity and dignity upon you.""**"
"5962","Abu al-Ashadd al-Sulami reported from his father, from his grandfather, who said he was the seventh of seven with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He commanded them, so a dirham was collected from each of them, and they bought a sacrifice for seven dirhams. They remarked that they had paid dearly for it. > **""The most excellent sacrifices are the dearest and the fattest.""** The grandfather continued that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ then commanded: one man held a leg, another a leg, another a hand, another a hand, another a horn, another a horn, the seventh slaughtered, and they all pronounced takbir over it."
"5963","Abdullah ibn Hisham (رضي الله عنه), who had met the Prophet ﷺ, reported that his mother brought him to the Prophet ﷺ, who wiped his head and supplicated for him. He used to sacrifice one sheep on behalf of all his household."
"5964","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) gave a concession concerning a man who buys a camel or sacrificial animal, then sells it and buys one fatter than it, permitting such exchange."
"5965","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the day of sacrifice and the day of breaking fast being with the congregation. > **""Sacrifice is on the day they sacrifice, and breaking fast is on the day they break fast.""**"
"5966","Abu Rafi' (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sacrificed two amlah, castrated rams. He declared one for whoever bore witness to tawhid and to his conveying the message, and the other for himself and his household. The narrator said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had thereby sufficed them the burden. > **""One of them is for whoever bore witness to tawhid and to my conveying, and the other is for me and my household.""**"
"5967","Abu Rafi', the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sacrificed, he would buy two fat, horned, amlah rams. When he had prayed and preached, one was brought to him in his prayer place and he slaughtered it, then said it was for his whole Ummah, then the other was brought and he slaughtered it for Muhammad and the family of Muhammad. He fed both to the poor, and he and his household ate from them. The narrator said years passed wherein none of Banu Hashim sacrificed, for Allah had sufficed them the cost and burden through the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""O Allah, this is for my Ummah altogether, whoever bore witness to You of tawhid and bore witness to me of conveying. O Allah, this is for Muhammad and the family of Muhammad.""**"
"5968","Abu Rafi' (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ slaughtered a ram, then declared it for himself and his Ummah. > **""This is for me and for my Ummah.""**"
"5969","Jabir ibn Abdillah (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was brought two horned, amlah, great, castrated rams. He laid one down and pronounced the name of Allah over it for Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, then laid the other down for Muhammad and his Ummah, for whoever bore witness of tawhid and of his conveying. > **""In the name of Allah, and Allah is Greatest. O Allah, for Muhammad and the family of Muhammad. In the name of Allah, and Allah is Greatest, for Muhammad and his Ummah, whoever bore witness to You of tawhid and bore witness to me of conveying.""**"
"5970","Abu Sa'id (رضي الله عنه) reported that on the Day of Sacrifice the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was brought two amlah rams. He mentioned one for Muhammad and his household, and the other for whoever of his Ummah had not sacrificed. > **""This is for Muhammad and his household. This is for whoever of my Ummah has not sacrificed.""**"
"5971","Abu al-Darda (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sacrificed two young, castrated rams, which had been gifted to him."
"5972","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sacrificed two horned, amlah rams. He brought one near and pronounced over it that it was for Muhammad and his household, from Allah and for Allah. He brought the other near for whoever of his Ummah affirmed His Oneness. > **""In the name of Allah, O Allah, from You and for You, this is for Muhammad and his household. In the name of Allah, O Allah, from You and for You, this is for whoever of my Ummah affirmed Your Oneness.""**"
"5973","Abu Talhah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ sacrificed two amlah rams, saying at the slaughter of the first that it was for Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, and at the slaughter of the second that it was for whoever of his Ummah believed in him and affirmed him. > **""For Muhammad and the family of Muhammad. For whoever of my Ummah believed in me and affirmed me.""**"
"5974","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sacrificed two horned, amlah rams: one for himself and his household, and the other for himself and whoever of his Ummah had not sacrificed."
"5975","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sacrificed a horned, large-eyed, sturdy ram."
"5976","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would sacrifice two amlah rams, placing his foot upon their sides when he intended to slaughter, and supplicating for acceptance from Muhammad. > **""O Allah, from You and for You. O Allah, accept from Muhammad.""**"
"5977","Hudhayfah ibn Usayd (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would bring near two amlah rams, slaughter one for Muhammad and the family of Muhammad, and bring the other near for his Ummah, for whoever bore witness to Allah of tawhid and to him of conveying. > **""O Allah, this is for Muhammad and the family of Muhammad. O Allah, this is for my Ummah, for whoever bore witness to You of tawhid and bore witness to me of conveying.""**"
"5978","Nu'man ibn Abi Fatimah (رضي الله عنه) reported that he bought a horned, large-eyed ram. The Prophet ﷺ saw it and remarked that it was as though it were the ram slaughtered by Ibrahim (عليه السلام). A man of the Ansar then bought one of that description for the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ took it and sacrificed it. > **""As though this ram is the one slaughtered by Ibrahim.""**"
"5979","Ali (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him to sacrifice two rams on his behalf, and he loves to do so. Al-Muharibi narrated in his hadith that he sacrificed two rams on his behalf: one for the Prophet ﷺ and the other for himself. When questioned about it, he replied that he ﷺ had commanded him, so he would never abandon it."
"5980","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade sacrificing at night."
"5981","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said his father had slaughtered his sacrifice before praying. The Prophet ﷺ instructed him to tell his father to pray and then slaughter. > **""Tell your father to pray, then slaughter.""**"
"5982","Jabir ibn Abdillah (رضي الله عنهما) reported that a man slaughtered a young goat before the Prophet ﷺ had prayed. The Prophet ﷺ declared it would not suffice for anyone after him, and forbade them to slaughter until they had prayed. > **""It will not suffice for anyone after you.""**"
"5983","Abu Burdah ibn Niyar (رضي الله عنه) reported that he attended Eid with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he left for prayer, his wife went to his sacrifice, slaughtered it, and prepared food from it. When he returned after the Prophet ﷺ had led them in prayer, she brought the prepared food. He asked whence it was, and she said she had slaughtered his sacrifice and prepared food so he could eat upon return. He feared this was improper, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and mentioned it. The Prophet ﷺ declared it of no account and commanded him to sacrifice anew. He sought a musinnah but could not find one. > **""It is nothing, so sacrifice anew. Seek a jadh' of sheep and sacrifice it.""** So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted him concession in the jadh' of sheep, and he sacrificed it when he had not found the musinnah."
"5984","Abu Juhayfah (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man slaughtered before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed on the Day of Sacrifice. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him it would not suffice for him. He said he had a jadh'ah with him. > **""It will not suffice for you. It will suffice for you, but will not suffice after you.""**"
"5985","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he spoke on the day of sacrifice concerning whoever had slaughtered before the prayer repeating his slaughter. > **""Whoever had slaughtered before the prayer, let him repeat his slaughter.""**"
"5986","Sahl ibn Hathmah (رضي الله عنه) reported that Abu Burdah ibn Niyar (رضي الله عنه) slaughtered his slaughter at dawn. When he had finished, he mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who told him that whoever slaughtered before the prayer, that is not the sacrifice; the sacrifice is only what is slaughtered after the prayer, and commanded him to go and sacrifice. He replied that he had found nothing for sacrifice and had only young goats. > **""Whoever slaughtered before the prayer, that is not the sacrifice; the sacrifice is only what is slaughtered after the prayer. Go and sacrifice. Go and sacrifice it, and there is no concession therein for anyone after you.""**"
"5987","Jubayr ibn Mut'im (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the places of standing and slaughter. > **""All of Arafat is a standing place, but keep away from Arafat. All of Muzdalifah is a standing place, but keep away from Muhassir. All ravines of Mina are a slaughter place, and all the days of tashriq are for slaughter.""**"
"5988","Jubayr ibn Mut'im (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ in the narration of al-Tabarani in al-Awsat. > **""All the days of tashriq are for slaughter.""**"
"5989","Abu al-Khayr reported from a man of the Ansar (رضي الله عنه) that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laid his sacrifice down to slaughter it, then asked the man to help him with his sacrifice, so he helped him. > **""Help me with my sacrifice.""**"
"5990","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning eating from one's sacrifice. > **""When one of you sacrifices, let him eat from his sacrifice.""**"
"5991","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Let every man eat from his sacrifice.""**"
"5992","Abdullah ibn Ata ibn Ibrahim, freedman of al-Zubayr, reported from his mother and his grandmother Umm Ata that they said they could still see al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (رضي الله عنه) when he came to them upon his white mule and said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden the Muslims to eat from the meat of their rites beyond three days. She asked how they should deal with what was gifted to them, and he replied that as for what was gifted, they might do with it as they wished. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has forbidden the Muslims to eat from the meat of their rites beyond three. As for what is gifted to you, do with it as you wish.""**"
"5993","Ali (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade visiting graves, and forbade certain vessels, and forbade keeping the meat of sacrifices after three days. He then permitted all three, explaining that graves remind of the Hereafter, and forbidding only what intoxicates, and permitting keeping whatever seemed fit. > **""I had forbidden you from visiting graves, so visit them, for they remind of the Hereafter. I had forbidden you vessels, so drink therein, and I forbid you all that intoxicates. I had forbidden you keeping the meat of sacrifices after three, so keep whatever seems fit to you.""**"
"5994","Zubayd reported that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (رضي الله عنه) came to his family and found a bowl of dried sacrificial meat, and refused to eat it. He went to Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man (رضي الله عنه), who informed him that the Prophet ﷺ had stood and first commanded them not to eat sacrifices beyond three days so that there would be room for others, then permitted it, forbidding the sale of the meat of hady and sacrifices, commanding eating, charity, and benefiting from skins without selling them, and permitting eating what one is fed thereof. > **""I had commanded you not to eat sacrifices beyond three days so that there would be room for you, and now I permit it for you, so eat thereof as you wish. Do not sell the meat of hady and sacrifices. Eat, give charity, benefit from their skins, and do not sell them. If you are fed of its meat, eat thereof if you wish. Now eat, trade, and store.""**"
"5995","Ibn Jurayj reported from Abu Sa'id and from Abu al-Zubayr from Jabir (رضي الله عنهما), though Abu al-Zubayr had not received the whole story, that Abu Qatadah (رضي الله عنه) came to his family and found a bowl of tharid made of dried sacrificial meat, and refused to eat it. He went to Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man (رضي الله عنه), who informed him that the Prophet ﷺ had stood among those performing Hajj and first commanded them, with the remainder like the previous narration. > **""I had commanded you.""**"
"5996","Abu Sa'id (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden them to eat the meat of their rites beyond three days. He went out on a journey, then returned to his family days after Adha. His wife brought him greens in which she had put dried meat. He asked whence the dried meat was, and she said from their sacrifices. He asked whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had not forbidden eating it beyond three, and she said he had granted concession to the people after that. He did not believe her until he sent to his brother Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man (رضي الله عنه), who was a Badri, to ask him. He sent back that she had spoken truthfully, and that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had granted concession to the Muslims in that, so he should eat his food."
"5997","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the abrogation of the prohibitions of graves, storing sacrificial meat beyond three, and vessels. > **""I had forbidden you from visiting graves, so visit them. I had forbidden you to store the meat of sacrifices beyond three, so store. I had forbidden you vessels, so prepare nabidh therein, but avoid every intoxicant.""**"
"5998","Sulayman ibn Abi Sulayman reported from his mother Umm Sulayman, both trustworthy, that she entered upon Aishah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ (رضي الله عنها), and asked her about the meat of sacrifices, noting the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden it then granted concession. Ali ibn Abi Talib (رضي الله عنه) had returned from a journey, and Fatimah (رضي الله عنها) brought him meat of her sacrifices. He asked whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had not forbidden it, and she said he had granted concession therein."
"5999","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he had forbidden nabidh of jars, keeping the meat of sacrifices beyond three days, and visiting graves. He then permitted nabidh in whatever vessels seemed fit, for vessels neither permit nor forbid, permitted keeping whatever seemed fit, and permitted visiting graves for they remind of the Hereafter. > **""I had forbidden you nabidh of jars, so prepare nabidh in whatever seems fit to you, for vessels neither permit anything nor forbid it. I had forbidden you keeping the meat of sacrifices beyond three, so keep whatever seems fit to you. I had forbidden you visiting graves, so visit them, for they remind of the Hereafter.""**"
"6000","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he had forbidden eating the meat of sacrifices after three days, and nabidh in jars, and visiting graves. Afterwards the Messenger of Allah ﷺ permitted eating as one wished, drinking from jars while every intoxicant is forbidden, and visiting graves without saying what angers Allah Almighty. > **""I had forbidden you the meat of sacrifices after three, so eat as you wish. I had forbidden you nabidh in jars, so drink, but every intoxicant is forbidden. I had forbidden you visiting graves, so visit them, but do not say what angers Allah Almighty.""**"
"6001","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he had forbidden nabidh of jars, visiting graves, and the meat of sacrifices. Vessels neither permit nor forbid, graves are to be visited for they soften the heart. Abdullah added in his narration the permission to eat and store as one wished. > **""I had forbidden you nabidh of jars. I had forbidden you visiting graves. I had forbidden you sacrifices. Verily vessels neither permit anything nor forbid it. Visit graves, for they soften the heart. I had forbidden you the meat of sacrifices, so eat and store as you wish.""**"
"6002","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that Quraysh sought permission of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning atirah, asking whether they should perform atirah in Rajab. He asked whether it was like the atirah of Jahiliyyah, permitting whoever wished to slaughter for Allah, eat, and give charity. Their atirah had been to slaughter, then take the blood of their slaughters and wipe it upon the heads of their idols. > **""An atirah like the atirah of Jahiliyyah? But whoever of you wishes to slaughter for Allah, and eat and give charity, let him do so.""**"
"6003","Abu al-Ushara reported from his father (رضي الله عنه) that the Prophet ﷺ was asked about atirah and deemed it good."
"6004","Samurah (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man from the Ansar came to the Prophet ﷺ seeking a ruling concerning what is lawful for a man and what is unlawful for him from his wealth, his rites of sacrifice, his livestock, his 'atrah and his fara', from the offspring of his camels and his sheep. > **""I permit for you the wholesome things, and I forbid for you the foul things, except that you become in need of food, so you may eat thereof until you become free of need. When you expect offspring, then sustain yourself with the meat of your livestock until your offspring arrives, or you expect rain which you deem forthcoming, then sustain yourself with the meat of your livestock until you reach it, or you expect provisions that you will obtain, then sustain yourself with the meat of your livestock until you reach them. And when you expect nothing of that, then feed your family as seems fit to you until you become free of need. When you have given your family their fill of evening milk, then avoid what of food has been forbidden to you. As for your wealth, it is all unrestricted, there is nothing unlawful therein, except that in the offspring of your camels there is a fara', and in the offspring of your sheep there is a fara', which your livestock nourish until it becomes independent, then if you wish feed it to your family, and if you wish give its meat in charity.""** The Prophet ﷺ further commanded him to offer an 'atirah from sheep, one 'atirah from every hundred."
"6005","Yazid ibn 'Abdillah al-Muzani reported from his father (رضي الله عنه) that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning the first offspring. > **""In camels there is a fara', and in sheep there is a fara'.""**"
"6006","'Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding the fara'ah from sheep, one from every five."
"6007","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, we used to offer the 'atirah in Jahiliyyah, so what do you command us? > **""Slaughter in whatever month it may be, and devote yourselves to Allah, and feed.""**"
"6008","Ibn 'Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ was asked about it on the Day of 'Arafah, meaning the 'atirah. > **""It is a right.""**"
